The assessment is at session_plan/assessment.md (about 10.7 KB). It isn't committed: session_plan/ is gitignored, and the previous session untracked it on purpose, so the commit step did nothing as expected.

The main finding: main is failing in CI, even though every evolve session reports success.
• The CI run on d0a30f13 (the Day 233 06:18 session) failed on two tests in tests/config_fifo.rs: regular_config_system_file_is_still_read and fifo_config_system_file_does_not_hang_and_is_named. Both stop with No API key found.
• Cause: the tests run yoyo --print-system-prompt, but parse_args (src/cli.rs ~1104) asks for an API key before the flag is acted on in src/main.rs:998. The test helper run_yoyo_env copies the runner's API keys into the child process. The evolve runner has ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set and CI doesn't, so both tests pass in the evolve session and fail in CI.
• Reproduced locally: env -u ANTHROPIC_API_KEY -u API_KEY cargo test --test config_fifo gives 8 passed, 2 failed.
• How far it spreads: I ran all 20 test files in tests/ with every API key unset. Only config_fifo failed, with the same 2 tests. I didn't run the unit tests inside src/ without keys.
• Two fixes, which can be combined:
  • Let --print-system-prompt run without an API key. It never calls a model, so asking for a key is a real annoyance for users too.
  • Make run_yoyo_env remove the key variables, as the sibling --continue test in the same file already does by passing a dummy key.
• How to verify: a plain cargo test on the evolve runner passes whether or not the fix is in. The check has to run with the keys unset, as above.

Other findings in the assessment:
• Still to do: item 3 of #1052 (the diff preview shown before write_file overwrites a file can still hang on a pipe) and #1053 (/todo board evidence can write over a file it failed to read).
• #1041 stays open on purpose: a renamed MCP server is still started twice.
• Ideas from the latest Claude Code release notes (v2.1.299–303):
  • Claude Code now recovers when the API rejects an MCP tool's input schema. yoyo has no such recovery, and I haven't tested what happens.
  • MCP server names that contain __ broke Claude Code's allow/deny rules. yoyo's prefix code can produce __ in a prefix, and I haven't checked whether that confuses its disallow rules.
  • Claude Code now hides skills whose names contain hidden or direction-changing characters.

  I saved these notes to yopedia.
